WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.210
From casting and direction, to props and wardrobe,
every little piece involved in a major motion

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:08.420
picture becomes gilded in history until the
end of time.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.460
But although the hours of footage passes by
a thousand professionally-trained eyes, sometimes

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.140
a wardrobe malfunction skips all of the censors
lands in the final cut.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.540
Whether no one noticed, or the picture was
already locked, these blockbusters hit the

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.090
big screen with wardrobe pieces that were
way out of place.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.829
Too bold for the background

00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:30.099
Steven Spielberg's Raiders of the Lost Ark
keenly depicts Egypt in the mid-1930s.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.540
"Who knows, in a thousand years, maybe even
you will be worth something."

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.280
"Ha ha ha ha"

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.600
But between the earth-toned trinkets and the
intricate tapestries that line the city markets,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.280
one guy totally blows the magic of cinema.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.830
Just behind Harrison Ford's Indiana Jones,
you'll spot a guy wearing blue jeans and a

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.380
T-shirt, casually passing by.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.960
A misplaced crew member, or a time-traveling
adventurer?

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:54.960
You decide.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.690
Undies from the future

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.390
Russell Crowe takes the lead in Ridley Scott's
Gladiator as a general bent on vengeance following

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:02.840
the murder of his family.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.560
"My name is Maximus Decimus Meridius, Commander
of the Armies of the North, General of the

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:13.420
Felix legions, and loyal servant to the true
emperor Marcus Aurelius."

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.420
But what ripped some viewers out of the moment,
however, was the wardrobe malfunction in the

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.509
middle of a fight scene — where Crowe's
Lycra shorts made a guest appearance from

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:22.549
under his tunic.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:25.600
Which, we suppose, is better than nothing.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.569
What time (period) is it?

00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:32.100
Starring industry heavyweights, and helmed
by Academy Award-winning filmmaker Edward,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.590
Glory earned well-deserved accolades when
it debuted in 1989.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:42.000
"If tomorrow we have to meet our Judgment
Day, our Heavenly Father, we want you to let

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.179
our folks know… uh, that we died facing
the enemy!"

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:50.060
Critics lauded the American Civil War-set
film for its breathtaking cinematography,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.560
and "enormous attention to period detail"
— praise which comes off as a bit ironic

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.289
today, considering the wardrobe piece that
made it to the theatrical cut.

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:02.820
We're pretty sure that digital time-telling
technology wouldn't have been around in 19th-century

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:03.820
America.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.399
But that wasn't the only time a timepiece
stole the scene.

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.640
A Rolex out of time

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.200
Bear with us, because this one is really nerdy.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.750
With its pitch-perfect soundtrack, superb
costume design, and fine-tuned set design,

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.950
David O. Russell's American Hustle provides
an excellent snapshot of the sights and sounds

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.110
of the '70s.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.090
The main cast seems to have walked straight
out of the disco-crazed decade, and so does

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.060
supporting actor Louis C.K. — except for
one detail.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.280
The Rolex C.K.'s character wears may look
super fancy, but it isn't era-appropriate.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.140
It's a GMT-Master II Rolex with a ceramic
bezel — first made around 2010.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.470
"Y'know we'll finish the ice story another
time young man."

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.570
The erroneous earpiece

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:49.220
Captain America: The First Avenger launched
star Chris Evans further into superstardom

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.120
and stands as one of the most well-loved Marvel
movies in history.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.340
"How do you feel?"

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.840
"Taller."

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.550
But in the midst of all the "pulpy action"
and "pleasantly retro vibe" that the critics

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:07.680
applauded, there's an historical slip-up that
surprisingly made it into the film.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.870
Right before Cap and his crew raid a HYDRA
train, Kenneth Choi's character is shown wearing

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:17.120
an unusually high-tech earpiece — which
definitely didn't exist during WWII.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.370
A little too much skin

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:24.320
Even if you've never seen Closer, you've certainly
seen Natalie Portman's character, the dancer

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.830
from America who arrives in London, and falls
for Jude Law's character.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.720
"Would you like me to stop being cheeky."

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.860
In the final cut, she only flaunts a little
skin.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.000
But thanks to a wardrobe malfunction, a lot
more is shown.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.340
While talking to Clive Owen, Portman's top
slips into "N-S-F-W" territory.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.770
Needless to say, it ended up in the movie
anyway.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:45.770
Thanks for watching!

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.320
